1
00:00:00,444 --> 00:00:07,281
H two adds a photograph to a saved item. This walkthrough uses a synthetic gemstone
illustration,

2
00:00:07,281 --> 00:00:14,119
so no personal photograph or embedded location information is involved. The item is
already saved with

3
00:00:14,119 --> 00:00:16,256
its name and storage location.

4
00:00:17,675 --> 00:00:24,460
Choose an image using the native picker. The browser resizes this camera sized image
before anything

5
00:00:24,460 --> 00:00:31,244
is uploaded. You can inspect the preview and the prepared file size. Embedded
metadata is removed,

6
00:00:31,244 --> 00:00:37,181
but anything visible in the photograph remains visible. Upload only when you are
ready.

7
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:45,279
The photograph is now saved. Reloading the page brings it back from private storage.
The gallery

8
00:00:45,279 --> 00:00:51,957
and compact list show the whole image without cropping. Another signed in browser
session can open

9
00:00:51,957 --> 00:00:53,627
the same saved photograph.

10
00:00:55,046 --> 00:01:01,848
Choose a replacement and review it beside the saved photograph. The current image
stays available while

11
00:01:01,848 --> 00:01:08,651
the replacement is prepared. Here we deliberately lose the response after the server
saves. Retry upload

12
00:01:08,651 --> 00:01:12,477
safely resolves the same request and confirms the replacement.

13
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:20,149
On a phone, the same photograph and actions remain available. Light and Dark
appearances use a

14
00:01:20,149 --> 00:01:26,399
neutral background around the whole image. You can recognize the piece and read its
storage location

15
00:01:26,399 --> 00:01:28,352
without returning to a desktop.

16
00:01:29,769 --> 00:01:37,567
Removing a photograph asks for confirmation. You can keep it, or confirm removal. The
item, its

17
00:01:37,567 --> 00:01:45,366
name, notes, and storage location remain in the collection. H two supports one
photograph per saved

18
00:01:45,366 --> 00:01:48,290
item, with explicit replacement and removal.
